{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310545,"nonce":"WzOdYAgnbRE50hclH5Rc8pfTFAw=","title":"FIXED (a0219c1) — llama-chat printed U+FFFD `�` before every word because BPETokenizer.byteDecode read input as raw bytes, not UTF-8 ...","message":"llama-chat with raw prompt `\"The capital city of France is\"` (post-bmm fix, post-reshape fix):\n- **before**: `'is� Paris,� which� is� located� in� the�'`\n- **after**:  `'is Paris, which is located in the capital'`\n\n\nTwo related bugs in the same byte-alphabet round-trip:\n\n1. **`byteDecoderArray()`** built `rev[code] = b` using `ch.charCodeAt(0)` as `code`. For spillover bytes, `ch = String.fromCharCode(next)` returns a multi-byte UTF-8 string in Rayzor (e.g. `String.fromCharCode(0x120)` → `c4 a0`). `charCodeAt(0)` returns the first byte (0xC4), not the codepoint (0x120). So the spillover entries scrambled the table — `rev[0xC4]` got overwritten with 0x20 (space) instead of staying 0xC4, and `rev[0x120]` was never written.\n\n2. **`byteDecode(s)`** walked the input string per-byte (`s.charCodeAt(i)` for `i in 0...s.length`). For ` Paris` (bytes `c4 a0 50 61 72 69 73`), it processed 7 separate \"codes\" instead of decoding the 2-byte UTF-8 sequence `c4 a0` as codepoint 0x120 first.\n\n\nBoth bugs are platform-specific. On Haxe targets where String is codepoint-indexed (cpp UCS-2, JS UTF-16, etc.) `charCodeAt(0)` of `String.fromCharCode(0x120)` returns 0x120, and the code would work. Rayzor's `runtime/src/haxe_string.rs:135 haxe_string_char_code_at` returns the raw byte at index, and `runtime/src/haxe_sys.rs:1028 haxe_string_from_char_code` UTF-8-encodes the codepoint. Both are correct individually; together they make codepoint-aware code subtly wrong.\n\n\nBoth helpers now decode UTF-8 explicitly:\n\n```haxe\nvar b0 = ch.charCodeAt(0);\nif (b0 \u003c 0x80) {\n    code = b0;\n} else if ((b0 \u0026 0xE0) == 0xC0 \u0026\u0026 len \u003e= 2) {\n    code = ((b0 \u0026 0x1F) \u003c\u003c 6) | (b1 \u0026 0x3F);\n} else if ((b0 \u0026 0xF0) == 0xE0 \u0026\u0026 len \u003e= 3) {\n    code = ((b0 \u0026 0x0F) \u003c\u003c 12) | ((b1 \u0026 0x3F) \u003c\u003c 6) | (b2 \u0026 0x3F);\n} else if ((b0 \u0026 0xF8) == 0xF0 \u0026\u0026 len \u003e= 4) {\n    code = ((b0 \u0026 0x07) \u003c\u003c 18) | ((b1 \u0026 0x3F) \u003c\u003c 12) | ((b2 \u0026 0x3F) \u003c\u003c 6) | (b3 \u0026 0x3F);\n}\n```\n\n`byteDecode`'s worst-case buffer widens from `n*3` to `n*4` for 4-byte spill cases.\n\n\n- Not a coherence bug — the model was emitting the correct ` Paris` token id (12366) all along. Only printing was broken.\n- Not a token-id bug — encode path was already correct because it walks the byte stream directly via `haxe.io.Bytes.ofString`.\n- Not specific to Llama 3 — affects any GPT-2-style byte-alphabet tokenizer on Rayzor.\n\n\nAny pure-Haxe code that wants to do codepoint-level work on UTF-8 strings in Rayzor must decode the UTF-8 sequence explicitly. `s.length` is byte count; `s.charCodeAt(i)` is i-th byte. `String.fromCharCode(\u003e=0x80)` emits multi-byte UTF-8. These contracts are useful for performance (no conversion cost) but trap callers who expect codepoint semantics.\n\nConsider a `StringTools.codePointAt(s, byteIndex)` helper if more sites need this — there are likely others (Unicode-normalising tokenisers, JSON escape handling, etc.).\n\n\n- [[project-llama-chat-coherence-phase-c]] — the bmm + reshape coherence push. The `�` was the *last* visible flaw in clean output; this fix completes the user-visible pipeline.\n- [[bugs-stringmap-null-get]] — unrelated, but the BPETokenizer header doc already cites it as the reason `rankOf` uses `exists()` over `get == null`.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_bpe_utf8_codepoint_walk` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}